1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,682
A cake changes size.

2
00:00:01,682 --> 00:00:02,399
Then flavor.

3
00:00:02,399 --> 00:00:03,473
Then the date.

4
00:00:03,473 --> 00:00:07,770
For a small business, one missed message can become an expensive mistake.

5
00:00:14,250 --> 00:00:16,855
Small businesses need more than another chatbot.

6
00:00:16,855 --> 00:00:22,437
Meet Order Guard: an AI sales and order operations agent, grounded in each seller’s business.

7
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:27,566
The customer asks for a chocolate cake.

8
00:00:27,566 --> 00:00:31,868
Meta Muse Spark understands the request and asks for the details it needs.

9
00:00:31,868 --> 00:00:35,177
A one kilogram cake for pickup is nine hundred rupees.

10
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:42,880
Now make it eggless, with floral decoration.

11
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:47,014
The backend applies the configured charges, and replaces the old quote.

12
00:00:47,014 --> 00:00:49,645
The new total is thirteen hundred rupees.

13
00:00:49,645 --> 00:00:51,899
Muse does not invent the price.

14
00:00:55,250 --> 00:00:58,293
A natural yes accepts this exact valid quote.

15
00:00:58,293 --> 00:01:00,955
The seller sees the saved commitment automatically.

16
00:01:00,955 --> 00:01:05,138
Acceptance does not verify payment, and it does not start production.

17
00:01:08,250 --> 00:01:11,565
Here is the difficult case, verified in Scenario Lab.

18
00:01:11,565 --> 00:01:15,248
Production has already started when the customer changes the cake.

19
00:01:15,248 --> 00:01:20,036
Order Guard preserves the frozen production instructions and creates one clear owner review.

20
00:01:20,036 --> 00:01:23,350
A request is never silently turned into a promise.

21
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:29,966
The same system works for a florist.

22
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:33,845
The seller describes the catalogue and policies in natural language.

23
00:01:33,845 --> 00:01:38,501
Muse proposes sourced draft rules; the seller reviews and publishes the configuration.

24
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:50,372
For Bloom and Company, the real verified order is eight hundred fifty nine rupees: roses, ribbon, a greeting card, and delivery.

25
00:01:50,372 --> 00:01:53,467
The accepted order keeps its original commercial terms.

26
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:01,203
After a published price change, a new quote is nine hundred fifty nine rupees.

27
00:02:01,203 --> 00:02:04,740
The earlier accepted order stays at eight hundred fifty nine.

28
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:05,802
History is preserved.

29
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:10,638
Muse interprets language and composes validated replies.

30
00:02:10,638 --> 00:02:14,026
Deterministic rules authorize prices, policies, and capacity.

31
00:02:14,026 --> 00:02:16,446
PostgreSQL persists sourced commitments atomically.

32
00:02:16,446 --> 00:02:23,706
One hundred fifty one backend tests pass, with eight complex scenarios verified using real Muse.

33
00:02:23,706 --> 00:02:29,998
The Instagram adapter is tested with synthetic events; live Instagram is not connected.

34
00:02:30,998 --> 00:02:32,040
Friendly conversations.

35
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:33,082
Reliable orders.

36
00:02:33,082 --> 00:02:35,687
Human judgment when it matters.

37
00:02:35,687 --> 00:02:36,729
Order Guard.

38
00:02:36,729 --> 00:02:37,771
Less inbox.

39
00:02:37,771 --> 00:02:38,813
More business.

